rknpu: publish the device only once it is built

rknpu_drm_probe() calls drm_dev_register() and assigns dev_private
afterwards. An open landing between the two gets a drm_device whose private
pointer is still NULL, and every GEM path dereferences it without checking.

The registration is also far too early in probe. It runs before power is on,
before devfreq, the power-off workqueue, the deferrable work, the IOMMU
domains, the cache scatter-gather tables, the debugger and the timer exist -
roughly sixty lines of setup that an ioctl arriving in that window would
reach half-finished. The misc device has the same problem.

Split the helper: rknpu_drm_alloc() creates the device and wires dev_private
both ways without publishing it, and rknpu_drm_register() publishes. The
allocation stays where the old call was, because the rest of probe hangs
things off the object; the registration moves to the end, after the last
subsystem is up. The misc device registers there too.

Nothing between the two touches rknpu_dev->drm_dev - it is only read in
remove - so the split is safe.

Failure before publication now releases the allocation with
rknpu_drm_free() rather than unregistering something that was never
registered, and a failure of the registration itself unwinds the debugger
and timer that publication now follows.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah DeRose-Wilson <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

